# Pull latest code
git pull

# Rebuild image
docker build -t my-node-app .

# Remove old container
docker stop my-app && docker rm my-app

# Run new container
docker run -d --name my-app -p 3000:3000 --env-file .env --restart unless-stopped my-node-app









# Stop the container
docker stop my-app && docker rm my-app

# Go to your app folder
cd /home/ubuntu/test

# Start with PM2 (reads .env via dotenv in the code)
pm2 start src/server.js --name my-app

# Save so it auto-starts on server reboot
pm2 save
pm2 startup


Use PM2 if you just want to run a Node.js app simply and quickly.

Use Docker if you want to ship the same code to any server without worrying about Node version, dependencies, or OS differences.

For your current project, PM2 is simpler. Docker becomes useful when you scale or deploy to multiple servers.



docker run -d --name my-app -p 3000:3000 --env-file .env --restart always my-node-app
